  "answer_text": "Great question! Computers and calculators use several clever mathematical techniques to compute square roots quickly and accurately. Here's a breakdown of the main methods:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd01 1. Iterative Guessing and Refining (The Core Idea)\n\nThe fundamental idea is simple: checking whether a number *x* is the square root of *a* is easy \u2014 just multiply it by itself and estimate the error |a \u2212 x\u00b2|. From there, you know whether to increase or decrease your guess. The algorithm keeps refining the guess until the error is tiny enough to be negligible.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcd0 2. Newton's Method (Newton-Raphson)\n\nThis is one of the most common techniques used by both calculators and software.\n\nOne key technique is **Newton's Method**, which finds the root of a function f(x) by performing the iteration x\u2099\u208a\u2081 = x\u2099 \u2212 f(x\u2099)/f\u2032(x\u2099), starting from an initial guess x\u2080.\n\nTo find \u221aa, you define f(x) = x\u00b2 \u2212 a, whose solution is exactly \u221aa. Substituting into Newton's method gives the formula: x\u2099\u208a\u2081 = \u00bdx\u2099 + a/(2x\u2099). This formula reaches high accuracy very quickly.\n\nIn fact, **the number of correct digits doubles with every step**, so using 15 digits of precision, only 5\u20136 steps are needed.\n\n---\n\n## \u26a1 3. Computing the Reciprocal Square Root (For Speed)\n\nModern processors can perform additions and multiplications very fast, but divisions are difficult and much slower. Consequently, if a square root algorithm uses division, it will be slow.\n\nA clever trick to remove all divisions is to calculate **1/\u221aa** instead of \u221aa directly! Once you have 1/\u221aa, you simply multiply it by *a* to get \u221aa. This allows the algorithm to rely only on fast multiplications.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccb 4. Lookup Tables for a Better Starting Guess\n\nDepending on the accuracy of the starting (or \"seed\") value, an algorithm will require fewer or more iterations to reach the desired precision. Because of this, Newton-Raphson-based algorithms often rely on **lookup tables** to determine more accurate seed values before the iterative process begins.\n\nThe use of lookup tables does have drawbacks, such as the need for large on-chip memory, so it is typically used only for high-precision (32-bit and greater) square root computations, where the lookup overhead is small compared to the iteration time saved.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd0d 5. Binary Search Method\n\nAnother approach, useful for simpler processors, is binary search. For example, to find \u221a25, you start with the range [0, 25], pick the middle value 12.5, square it to get 156.25 (too high), so the root must be smaller \u2014 narrowing the range to [0, 12.5]. This process repeats, halving the search range each time, until the answer is precise enough.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\uddc2\ufe0f Summary of Algorithm Classes\n\nIn general, three classes of algorithms for square root calculation are currently in use: **(a) subtractive algorithms**, **(b) multiplicative algorithms**, and **(c) divisive algorithms**. The method employed depends on the needed accuracy and the available tools and computational power.\n\nIn short, it's a combination of **smart mathematics** (like Newton's Method), **hardware optimization** (avoiding slow division), and **clever shortcuts** (like lookup tables) that allows computers and calculators to compute square roots so rapidly and accurately!",
